<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1116–<a href="/years/1198">1198</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Rory O'Connor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ruaidri Ua Conchobair was king of Connacht and the last high king of Ireland, recognised from 1166. The Anglo-Norman invasion that followed undermined his position, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-windsor-1175">Treaty of Windsor</a> in 1175 he accepted Henry II's overlordship. He later retired to the abbey of Cong, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruaidri-ua-conchobair.jpg" alt="Ruaidri Ua Conchobair" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ClintMalpaso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rory_O%27Connor_Stone_Carving.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ruaidrí was one of more than twenty sons of King Tairrdelbach Ua Conchobair, and with his sister Mór the only child of his father's third wife, Cailech Dé Ní Eidin of Aidhne. His brother Conchobar was the designated heir. In 1136 Ruaidrí and his brother Aedh rebelled; Aedh was blinded on their father's orders, but Ruaidrí was protected by Muireadhach Ua Dubhthaigh, Archbishop of Connacht. After a second rebellion in 1143 he was imprisoned for about a year, and freed only after Conchobar was killed in Mide in 1144.</p><p>Raids on Tighearnán Ua Ruairc in 1146 and the killing of his father's nephew Domnall Ua Conchobar in 1150 raised his standing, and the arrest of the new heir, Donnell Mór Mideach, in 1151 left him his father's heir. In 1153 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-mac-lochlainn">Muirchertach Mac Lochlainn</a>'s northern army surprised and defeated him at Fordruim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On his father's death in 1156 Ruaidrí became king of Connacht without opposition, arresting three brothers as a precaution; one, Brian Breifneach, was blinded. Mac Lochlainn claimed the high kingship, and Ruaidrí answered his campaigns by burning the country around Strabane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> and undoing his settlement of Munster. When Mac Lochlainn died in 1166 Ruaidrí was inaugurated as high king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, held the Óenach Tailten, expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Dermot MacMurrough</a> from Leinster and took hostages from the major kings.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> who came to restore MacMurrough took much of Leinster and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a>. Ruaidrí raised a large army, said to number sixty thousand, burned Norman castles at Trim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1171, but was routed when the defenders attacked his resting camp. He kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a>, and his forces destroyed a Norman expedition into Munster at the Battle of Thurles. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-windsor-1175">Treaty of Windsor</a>, Henry II did not restrain his barons nor Ruaidrí the lesser Irish kings, and the fighting went on.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ruaidrí abdicated in 1183 but twice returned to rule briefly, and died on 2 December 1198. His sons' lines lost the kingship: the last of them to rule Connacht, Aedh mac Ruaidri, died in 1233, and the Annals of Connacht explain the loss as punishment for Ruaidrí's refusal to give up adultery when the pope offered Ireland to his descendants. Later kings of Connacht descended from his younger brother, Cathal Crobhdearg Ua Conchobair.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</b>, the last high king, accepted Henry II's overlordship in the Treaty of Windsor of 1175, after the Norman invasion and the creation of the Lordship of Ireland; he died in 1198.</blockquote><a href="/state/high-kingship-of-ireland">High Kingship of Ireland</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Ruaidrí</b> became the last High King of Ireland and agreed the Treaty of Windsor with Henry II in 1175.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Kingdom of Connacht</a></li><li><blockquote>De Lacy settled English tenants, founded towns and abbeys and married a daughter of <b>Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</b>, the high king.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-meath">Lordship of Meath</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tuam, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Cong, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.52,-8.85&amp;year=1169" aria-label="Tuam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4353 324 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ruaidri Ua Conchobair"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4803 594h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4803 594h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.52,-8.85&amp;year=1169">What was here in 1169? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
